WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.314
Welcome to OurWater.

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:08.386
After this video you can open the check dam calculator and
read how much water went into the ground.

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:13.921
The check dam calculator is in the Tool
marketplace, in the Advanced menu.

00:00:14.041 --> 00:00:17.833
It shows how much water a check dam sent into
the ground.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.834
Press Open on its card.

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:22.826
No card?

00:00:22.946 --> 00:00:25.346
Ask a Tool Admin to add you.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:33.134
There are five steps: your data, the check dam, the
pond survey, the daily readings, and the result.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.862
Show explanations adds a short note under
every box.

00:00:37.982 --> 00:00:39.854
I will leave them on.

00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:42.819
Step one is your data.

00:00:42.939 --> 00:00:46.731
On the left, use measurements already in
OurWater.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.422
Choose your check dam.

00:00:49.542 --> 00:00:52.398
Each one in the list is a check dam station.

00:00:53.217 --> 00:01:00.369
Say where the rain comes from: this dam, a nearby rain
gauge with its distance in kilometres, or no rain.

00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:03.055
Then the dates.

00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:07.735
OurWater fills in the days this dam has
measurements, up to a year.

00:01:07.855 --> 00:01:09.727
You can narrow them.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.806
No measurements in OurWater?

00:01:12.926 --> 00:01:16.214
Drop in your own spreadsheet on the right
instead.

00:01:17.017 --> 00:01:19.441
Press Load into the calculator.

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:23.905
This line says how many days came in, and step
two opens.

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:27.237
Step two is the dam itself.

00:01:27.357 --> 00:01:30.861
You measure these once, and OurWater keeps
them.

00:01:30.981 --> 00:01:32.853
Check each box.

00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:38.339
First, the level of the zero mark on your
gauge board, in metres.

00:01:38.459 --> 00:01:41.747
Use one fixed starting point for every level.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:49.654
Next, the spillway, where extra water pours over the
dam: its level and its length, both in metres.

00:01:50.483 --> 00:01:55.883
Evaporation, in millimetres a day, is the
water the sun takes from the pond.

00:01:56.003 --> 00:01:59.723
Catchment area, in hectares, is optional.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.390
In Advanced view, two weir boxes are for
experts.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.222
The usual values are already there.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.762
Step three, the pond survey.

00:02:10.882 --> 00:02:15.754
For each water level in metres, write the
water area in square metres.

00:02:15.874 --> 00:02:19.402
Start at zero, and go up past the spillway.

00:02:20.217 --> 00:02:23.577
The Volume column and these charts are worked
out for you.

00:02:23.697 --> 00:02:26.193
Press Add a level for more rows.

00:02:27.017 --> 00:02:29.777
Step four, the daily readings.

00:02:29.897 --> 00:02:34.961
This note says fifty days of water level were
loaded, and rain on eight of them.

00:02:35.081 --> 00:02:37.721
No rain record counts as dry.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.406
The calculator also checks the readings.

00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:45.798
On four days the water did not fall, though it
hardly rained.

00:02:45.918 --> 00:02:49.158
Press the suggested level if a reading looks
wrong.

00:02:49.983 --> 00:02:55.647
Each row is a day: the gauge reading in
centimetres, and the rainfall in millimetres.

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:58.335
Leave rain empty on dry days.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.374
Water level, in metres, is what the calculator
uses.

00:03:03.494 --> 00:03:06.158
Notes flag a day that was filled in.

00:03:06.278 --> 00:03:09.278
Level override lets you correct a day.

00:03:10.083 --> 00:03:11.955
Press See the result.

00:03:12.075 --> 00:03:18.075
In fifty days, 4,753 cubic metres soaked into
the ground.

00:03:18.195 --> 00:03:24.435
That is 76.9 percent of the 6,179 cubic metres
that flowed in.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.962
A cubic metre is a thousand litres.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:31.994
So about four point eight million litres went
into the ground.

00:03:32.817 --> 00:03:35.121
Charts show where the water went.

00:03:35.241 --> 00:03:40.713
Of every hundred litres, about seventy-seven
soaked in and twenty-three evaporated.

00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:44.769
None spilled, because the water never reached
the spillway.

00:03:45.583 --> 00:03:50.743
Slide this bar, or press Play the season, to
watch the water rise and fall.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:56.998
These charts show the rain, the pond level,
and where the water went, day by day.

00:03:57.817 --> 00:04:02.473
Infiltration rate is a quick second answer
from the dry days: here,

00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:06.361
about fourteen millimetres of water soaking in
each day.

00:04:07.183 --> 00:04:12.127
How it works is a short course in six lessons,
using your own measurements.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.526
Press Download the PDF report to keep the
answer.

00:04:16.646 --> 00:04:19.742
You can print it or share it with your village
group.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:24.150
Download the numbers gives a spreadsheet of
every day.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.542
Press any step to change a number.

00:04:27.662 --> 00:04:32.366
The dam's levels stay with the station, so the
next person starts from them.

00:04:33.183 --> 00:04:35.655
That is the check dam calculator.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.439
Thanks for helping look after our water.
